fix(pip): Keep the reticle at the 3DSS scale, and hold the upscaler boost while aiming

Two behaviours the player reads directly, both from comparing what 3DSS does with what the PiP
path does, since the two mods ship the SAME reticle shader (identical hashes) and differ only in
the material (.s: s_second_vp = $user$viewport2 and the scopelense(3) marker) and in the uniforms
the engine publishes.

Reticle at maximum power. The shader computes current_zoom differently per path:
  3DSS: max(min_scope_zoom_factor * 0.75 / fFOV, 1), which is 1 for every optic in this pack, so
        the reticle is drawn at its texture scale and never magnified, whatever s3ds_ffp says.
  PiP:  fFOV / lens FOV, i.e. the full sight power, so an optic marked s3ds_ffp = 1 - the multi
        power ones, marchf for instance - has its reticle scaled by up to twenty odd times.
The reticle art is authored for the 3DSS scale and there is no per power art to magnify, so the
faithful first focal plane behaviour produced the cross tens of times too thick that the player
reported. The engine now selects the SFP branch the shader already has for exactly this case
(s3ds_param_1.w) while it is providing the second viewport, so the reticle keeps the scale the
player is used to. This is a deliberate deviation from the pack's FFP declaration, taken because
the declaration cannot be honoured with this art.

Upscaler resolution while aiming. ScopeResolutionRequested() returned false as soon as the PiP
path was live ("the override has nothing left to buy - the scope frame renders at that set's own
extent"). That is what makes the small sight go pixelated after a while: the boost could not even
be requested while the lens was up, so it only survived on ps_r__upscaler_scope_delay and then
fell back to the default upscaler scale mid aim. The lens is what the player is looking at and it
is magnified into the aperture, so the internal resolution is what its pixels are made of; the
second viewport now requests the boost and the idle timer only covers the other triggers.

The per pass diagnostic gained rsw=, the extent the pass actually renders the scene at. It is what
separates the two mechanisms: RenderScreenWidth() is the upscaler's size for the main view and the
target's own extent for the second viewport, while the lens texture is display sized in both
cases, so texture dimensions alone cannot tell which one a lens image came from.